{"name":"Pilot Blue","hex":"#006981","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pilot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who steers a ship, a helmsman.","A person who knows well the depths, shoals, and currents of a harbor or coastal area, who is hired by a vessel to help navigate the harbor or coast."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made or used as a test or demonstration of capability.","Used to control or activate another device."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pilot"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aircraft pilot","blocks":[{"text":"An aircraft pilot is an individual who controls an aircraft's flight by operating its directional controls. Other aircrew members, such as navigators and flight engineers, are also considered aviators because they assist in operating the aircraft’s navigation and engine systems. Aircrew members like drone operators, flight attendants, mechanics, and ground crew are not classified as aviators."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Lt_Mike_Hunter_1.jpg/330px-Lt_Mike_Hunter_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aircraft_pilot","wikidata":"Q2095549"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Transaero_777_landing_at_Sharm-el-Sheikh_Pereslavtsev.jpg/500px-Transaero_777_landing_at_Sharm-el-Sheikh_Pereslavtsev.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Transaero_777_landing_at_Sharm-el-Sheikh_Pereslavtsev.jpg/500px-Transaero_777_landing_at_Sharm-el-Sheikh_Pereslavtsev.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pilots landing a Boeing 777","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATransaero_777_landing_at_Sharm-el-Sheikh_Pereslavtsev.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/USAF_pilot.jpg/500px-USAF_pilot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/USAF_pilot.jpg/1280px-USAF_pilot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/USAF_pilot.jpg/500px-USAF_pilot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A U.S. Air Force F-16 pilot in flight","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUSAF_pilot.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Marcel_Loui